{"war":"taiping-rebellion","start":"1851-01-11","end":"1864-07-31","note":"Taiping-held ground is drawn as occupied areas; a moving area marks ground held by a marching army, not a conquest. Edges are approximate.","baseYears":[{"start":"1853-01-01","end":"1864-12-31","year":1852,"why":"the map's yearly Taiping outline is coarse and often wrong (it keeps Wuchang Taiping until 1861 and draws Shanghai as Taiping from 1861), so the storyboard draws 1852 Qing borders and shows Taiping control with its own dated areas"}],"chapters":[{"id":"guangxi","title":"Rising in Guangxi","start":"1851-01-11","end":"1852-04-05","bbox":[108.9,22.6,111.5,25.2],"arrows":["Taiping break out from Jintian and march to Yong'an (1851)"],"text":"In the poor, famine-struck hills of Guangxi, Hong Xiuquan's God Worshipping Society had grown into a movement of tens of thousands. On 11 January 1851 it rose at Jintian and proclaimed the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom. Surrounded by much larger Qing forces, the rebels fought their way north and took the town of Yong'an in September, where they held out for months."},{"id":"hunan","title":"The march to the Yangtze","start":"1852-04-05","end":"1853-01-20","bbox":[109.5,24.6,115.2,31.5],"arrows":["Taiping break out of Yong'an and march on Guilin (April 1852)","Taiping march north into Hunan (June–August 1852)","Taiping march on Changsha (September 1852)","Taiping descend the Xiang River to the Yangtze and Wuchang (December 1852)"],"text":"In 1852 the Taiping broke out of Yong'an, failed to take Guilin and marched north into Hunan. A militia ambush at Suoyi ford on the Xiang River cost them about a fifth of their army, and Changsha held out against a siege from September to November. The rebels then seized thousands of boats at Yuezhou, reached the Yangtze and took Wuchang on 12 January 1853."},{"id":"downriver","title":"Down the Yangtze to Nanjing","start":"1853-01-21","end":"1853-04-30","bbox":[113.8,29.3,120.2,32.8],"arrows":["Taiping sail down the Yangtze to Nanjing (February–March 1853)"],"text":"Instead of marching on Beijing, the Taiping leaders sailed east down the Yangtze with an army that had grown to hundreds of thousands. They passed Jiujiang and took Anqing in February, reached Nanjing on 6 March and broke into it on 19 March 1853, killing the Manchu garrison and its families. Hong made Nanjing his Heavenly Capital, and Taiping troops crossed the river to occupy Yangzhou."},{"id":"northern","title":"The Northern Expedition","start":"1853-05-08","end":"1855-05-31","bbox":[110.8,31.0,120.2,40.3],"arrows":["Northern Expedition marches on Beijing (May–October 1853)","Northern Expedition retreats south (February 1854)"],"text":"In May 1853 a Taiping army marched north from Yangzhou to take Beijing. It crossed the Yellow River but wasted two months besieging Huaiqing, then turned through Shanxi into Zhili and in late October stopped near Tianjin instead of attacking the capital. Freezing and hungry, it retreated in February 1854 into fortified villages, where Qing armies wore it down; by spring 1855 its leaders had been captured and the army destroyed."},{"id":"western","title":"The Western Expedition and the Xiang Army","start":"1853-05-19","end":"1856-05-31","bbox":[112.0,27.3,119.9,32.7],"arrows":["Western Expedition sails up the Yangtze (1853)","Xiang Army advances down the Yangtze to Wuchang (1854)"],"text":"A second Taiping army sailed up the Yangtze in 1853, retaking Anqing and Jiujiang and conquering much of Anhui, including Luzhou (Hefei), in January 1854. In Hunan, Zeng Guofan's new Xiang Army beat a Taiping force at Xiangtan in May 1854 and retook Wuchang in October. The Taiping struck back, recapturing Wuchang in April 1855 and overrunning much of Jiangxi."},{"id":"tianjing","title":"Murder in the Heavenly Capital","start":"1856-06-01","end":"1858-12-31","bbox":[113.8,28.0,119.9,32.7],"arrows":["Xiang Army advances on Jiujiang (1857–1858)","Li Xubin's column advances into central Anhui (September–November 1858)"],"text":"In June 1856 the Taiping broke the Qing siege camp outside Nanjing, but in September the East King Yang Xiuqing and thousands of his followers were massacred in a power struggle, and Shi Dakai later left with his army. The Qing took Wuchang for good that December and Jiujiang in May 1858, clearing Jiangxi. A new generation of Taiping generals struck back, destroying an elite Xiang Army force at Sanhe in November 1858."},{"id":"east-1860","title":"Breakout to the east","start":"1860-02-01","end":"1860-09-30","bbox":[117.8,29.8,122.2,32.7],"arrows":["Li Xiucheng's raid on Hangzhou (February–March 1860)","Taiping drive east down the Grand Canal (May–June 1860)"],"text":"With a rebuilt Qing army again besieging Nanjing, Li Xiucheng raided Hangzhou in March 1860 to draw it away, then turned back and routed it in May. The Taiping poured east down the Grand Canal, taking Changzhou on 26 May and the rich city of Suzhou on 2 June. In August an attack on Shanghai was beaten off by Qing troops and foreign-officered units."},{"id":"anqing","title":"The siege of Anqing","start":"1860-09-01","end":"1861-09-30","bbox":[115.5,29.5,118.9,32.7],"arrows":["Xiang Army advances on Anqing (1860)"],"text":"While the Taiping fought in the east, Zeng Guofan struck at Anqing, their great fortress upstream of Nanjing. His brother Zeng Guoquan besieged it from September 1860, and a British blockade stopped foreign merchants selling the city rice. Chen Yucheng's relief attempts failed, and on 5 September 1861 the Xiang Army breached the walls of the starving city, opening the river to Nanjing."},{"id":"zhejiang","title":"Zhejiang and Shanghai","start":"1861-10-01","end":"1862-10-31","bbox":[116.7,28.3,122.3,32.9],"arrows":["Taiping eastern offensive into Zhejiang (late 1861)"],"text":"In a last offensive the Taiping took Ningbo on 9 December and Hangzhou on 31 December 1861, and in January 1862 closed in on Shanghai. Britain and France now abandoned neutrality: with Li Hongzhang's Huai Army and Frederick Townsend Ward's Ever Victorious Army they cleared the country around Shanghai, and Ningbo was retaken on 10 May. Ward was mortally wounded at Cixi in September."},{"id":"jiangsu","title":"The reconquest of Jiangsu","start":"1862-11-01","end":"1864-05-15","bbox":[118.3,30.0,121.6,32.6],"arrows":["Xiang Army descends the Yangtze to Nanjing (spring 1862)","Huai Army and Ever Victorious Army advance on Suzhou (1863)","Advance on Changzhou (1864)"],"text":"Since May 1862 Zeng Guoquan's Xiang Army had been dug in outside Nanjing, and in June 1863 it took the last Taiping positions on the north bank. In the east, Li Hongzhang's Huai Army and the Ever Victorious Army under Charles Gordon took Kunshan in May 1863 and Suzhou in December, and Zuo Zongtang retook Hangzhou. Changzhou fell on 11 May 1864, leaving Nanjing almost alone."},{"id":"nanjing","title":"The fall of Nanjing","start":"1864-03-01","end":"1864-07-31","bbox":[118.4,31.8,119.2,32.35],"arrows":[],"text":"By March 1864 the Xiang Army had taken the Purple Mountain and completed the encirclement of Nanjing. Hong Xiuquan refused to leave and died on 1 June after eating wild plants. On 19 July a mine brought down a stretch of the wall and the Xiang Army stormed the city, killing, looting and burning for days. The Heavenly Kingdom was finished, though Taiping remnants fought on in the south until 1866."}],"fronts":[],"occupations":[{"name":"Taiping army on the march (1851–1853)","side":1,"keys":[{"date":"1851-01-11","coords":[[110.413,23.5],[110.39,23.597],[110.322,23.679],[110.22,23.734],[110.1,23.753],[109.98,23.734],[109.878,23.679],[109.81,23.597],[109.787,23.5],[109.81,23.403],[109.878,23.321],[109.98,23.266],[110.1,23.247],[110.22,23.266],[110.322,23.321],[110.39,23.403]]},{"date":"1851-06-01","coords":[[110.094,23.75],[110.072,23.854],[110.008,23.942],[109.913,24.001],[109.8,24.021],[109.687,24.001],[109.592,23.942],[109.528,23.854],[109.506,23.75],[109.528,23.646],[109.592,23.55